 the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79168/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79168"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272/selection/869e92fa37d662618b5776b5688120805f6a4ef64944d68a09668a86e88acb88" :answer/id #uuid "fe492f17-f98a-58e9-a0f3-1bf974a30c09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272/selection/bc3e76ff6c2251409d67bd4d44714df545c2e1406f35c243db21f791e2a3cb52" :answer/id #uuid "1e8903b3-20f7-5a33-88ad-63306bbb66a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" :answer/id #uuid "a0478b04-03d7-5829-9d4d-2675149472c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" :answer/id #uuid "93a4e948-6c89-5217-ad40-26a1c004aa4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e" :answer/id #uuid "62530a29-1774-564f-a02d-e05394fc8d49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcfb8d8-8f15-5e60-b657-5377191adc26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79272/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-79272/selection/869e92fa37d662618b5776b5688120805f6a4ef64944d68a09668a86e88acb88" "q-79272/selection/bc3e76ff6c2251409d67bd4d44714df545c2e1406f35c243db21f791e2a3cb52" "q-79272/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" "q-79272/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" "q-79272/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79272" :question/id #uuid "eb927d29-41e2-540b-8f69-95f193bb1d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79272" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/15dd47ff2ba433da8ad8f6ed25bfa984806e84e4150c0a56697526116cc41431.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=1.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/ddd66398b45c22772af257d6856454b63fde930253090329f99383e4e063fff4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79272/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79272"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793/selection/255f0c1062a3ba8e6335e3a16bde128be35df4cdfa239a398ec3aa43ed336b87" :answer/id #uuid "7d174746-1906-5cb2-bcc2-df3c3de0d5b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793/selection/aa2927c6e45ed290c1e8127cc4cd1080038ac348dc7fbfa5f0b6ebd838b4bc95" :answer/id #uuid "4d50a8d7-79de-5a02-b882-6e32e813ec13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793/selection/b5398d094f0d6f54a19341be7f60ba015a19751802caacfcfb24dd207ddf5b45" :answer/id #uuid "7b2851df-526e-537b-8bd7-e5e7ad7d157b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793/selection/c6f2a589a571f8d1f8f445cec78f27f6000b8faea9aea8caeacb0961869a4da9" :answer/id #uuid "c777c3f2-4e38-54c3-a7f1-0b4bd008fed3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793/selection/1bdd1feaf06af021d5cf8d9ccf996a9cb59e0a5139cbeb455a21f506477dd93b" :answer/id #uuid "abb3ba92-5e0d-5204-a3e0-6ee9079fa1e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5376308a-a84d-5a9f-94db-1ec29dc13d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15793/selection/b5398d094f0d6f54a19341be7f60ba015a19751802caacfcfb24dd207ddf5b45" :answer-field/choices ["q-15793/selection/255f0c1062a3ba8e6335e3a16bde128be35df4cdfa239a398ec3aa43ed336b87" "q-15793/selection/aa2927c6e45ed290c1e8127cc4cd1080038ac348dc7fbfa5f0b6ebd838b4bc95" "q-15793/selection/b5398d094f0d6f54a19341be7f60ba015a19751802caacfcfb24dd207ddf5b45" "q-15793/selection/c6f2a589a571f8d1f8f445cec78f27f6000b8faea9aea8caeacb0961869a4da9" "q-15793/selection/1bdd1feaf06af021d5cf8d9ccf996a9cb59e0a5139cbeb455a21f506477dd93b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15793" :question/id #uuid "b4492fb2-623c-509b-a905-5a08f6e893d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15793" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-15793/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15793"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1700e311-fdbb-5249-8ed2-be0517c8158f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5944889b-3977-56df-bc2a-70ba1591264d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321986/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-321986/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "b95a4f0b-1788-547f-b86e-4fdec25486f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2ff9e7-3827-59c8-887f-eab8e33ecc23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321986/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-321986/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986/field-3/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "66900aa4-6091-5a03-a8d4-8375dab94c4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "095831ff-75ba-502f-a387-4d813d1ba657" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321986/field-3/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer-field/choices ["q-321986/field-3/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321986" :question/id #uuid "4ee65cb6-ef5d-5634-9e99-c2bd8cbc9a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-321986" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-321986/field-1" "q-321986/field-2" "q-321986/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321986"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696/selection/a778fce84cab54c11d57c8eff4de3e4bf951675b7a79c7be357c799cfc4e41c8" :answer/id #uuid "bc51c0ce-5dfa-5202-b780-9e61826a191e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696/selection/b44e514c845258a13d898dbb7bd578f29b12f846a0d31252f0a61df7be3a9dd6" :answer/id #uuid "f0e2bbaf-d7a6-5d00-a992-9aec726e8547" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696/selection/5a24d3f5a93983417507f377e1b9198d2fd95105388978eef6f664a01f9bb351" :answer/id #uuid "fc10318e-d2a3-56ed-aeab-ff09360174b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696/selection/d3f7167d12557f381c1e8c32f3ef70e2b14bb0c3b0559a43220827b1bcaa5cb9" :answer/id #uuid "c16ea69c-7a27-5c2b-887b-d26306361e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696/selection/54228fa1ec1d98dfdb2fc209d8692e3c19f9ea1f9381c9017b52ca566761c713" :answer/id #uuid "2c6a2a01-bb93-5bca-a7ae-d7ce6bd27b75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "687d1247-73e3-5a10-87fb-3cb9095cb3f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106696/selection/b44e514c845258a13d898dbb7bd578f29b12f846a0d31252f0a61df7be3a9dd6" :answer-field/choices ["q-106696/selection/a778fce84cab54c11d57c8eff4de3e4bf951675b7a79c7be357c799cfc4e41c8" "q-106696/selection/b44e514c845258a13d898dbb7bd578f29b12f846a0d31252f0a61df7be3a9dd6" "q-106696/selection/5a24d3f5a93983417507f377e1b9198d2fd95105388978eef6f664a01f9bb351" "q-106696/selection/d3f7167d12557f381c1e8c32f3ef70e2b14bb0c3b0559a43220827b1bcaa5cb9" "q-106696/selection/54228fa1ec1d98dfdb2fc209d8692e3c19f9ea1f9381c9017b52ca566761c713"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106696" :question/id #uuid "92c2868f-7499-5783-bdd8-0dc234cb675c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106696" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106696/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106696"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222/selection/b9e0d62e05ab8754f47aa2cd76c5e8d3aa4d7261c50bdf5e12f06c01201dd787" :answer/id #uuid "0120083a-b2e6-57aa-84f1-e48ee635d4d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222/selection/d565dcef85b2cd349cc398e351d35a49769c1f9cafbb872e1a26ce85ceb56500" :answer/id #uuid "dd2dff0d-e8b1-55b2-b8c9-9ea04488b826" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222/selection/a4b62fdd752061eec6ae877e5277ca3636c1d2fa2a1835965d2b7c162d3c4129" :answer/id #uuid "52698a0c-da5d-5860-9902-5df49ac2439e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222/selection/e7c86b646008597cf8f34d9816dbce51bb3078a45307aad75fa5b0e49161768a" :answer/id #uuid "25fb9489-f74a-51e4-b8fd-4e60877c5716" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222/selection/a8244e7d820220e6f8524b7e5be5bab20d3baf8fc749a3b20c9c6dc13d4402ba" :answer/id #uuid "b5684018-b4fa-5838-a870-3c8045a0b4ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ae94d7-2c1b-5dca-9725-f27bbbe476f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79222/selection/b9e0d62e05ab8754f47aa2cd76c5e8d3aa4d7261c50bdf5e12f06c01201dd787" :answer-field/choices ["q-79222/selection/b9e0d62e05ab8754f47aa2cd76c5e8d3aa4d7261c50bdf5e12f06c01201dd787" "q-79222/selection/d565dcef85b2cd349cc398e351d35a49769c1f9cafbb872e1a26ce85ceb56500" "q-79222/selection/a4b62fdd752061eec6ae877e5277ca3636c1d2fa2a1835965d2b7c162d3c4129" "q-79222/selection/e7c86b646008597cf8f34d9816dbce51bb3078a45307aad75fa5b0e49161768a" "q-79222/selection/a8244e7d820220e6f8524b7e5be5bab20d3baf8fc749a3b20c9c6dc13d4402ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79222" :question/id #uuid "d6f537a3-5f7e-5f24-bef9-d798370c0a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-79222" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-5}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79222/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79222"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5374"] :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6779"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
